\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {19}{\ignorespaces Forward and backward pass of the two-dimensional convolution, simplified from the student's implementation. The code is the implementation of the equations and not a substitute for them: \texttt  {out} is Equation~\ref {eq:rec-im2col}, \texttt  {dw}, \texttt  {db} and \texttt  {dCols} are the three gradients of Equation~\ref {eq:rec-convbw}, and \texttt  {Col2Im} is the scatter-add of Equation~\ref {eq:rec-col2im}. The batch dimension $B$ is handled by a single broadcasted matrix product (\texttt  {@}) rather than a Python loop over samples, which was measured to be about 35\,\% faster than an equivalent per-sample loop for a realistic training batch.}}{54}{figure.caption.69}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:rec-conv2d}{{19}{54}{Forward and backward pass of the two-dimensional convolution, simplified from the student's implementation. The code is the implementation of the equations and not a substitute for them: \texttt {out} is Equation~\ref {eq:rec-im2col}, \texttt {dw}, \texttt {db} and \texttt {dCols} are the three gradients of Equation~\ref {eq:rec-convbw}, and \texttt {Col2Im} is the scatter-add of Equation~\ref {eq:rec-col2im}. The batch dimension $B$ is handled by a single broadcasted matrix product (\texttt {@}) rather than a Python loop over samples, which was measured to be about 35\,\% faster than an equivalent per-sample loop for a realistic training batch}{figure.caption.69}{}}
